1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,115
In lecture two we look at shape before numbers:

2
00:00:04,115 --> 00:00:09,107
distributions, summary statistics, and robust statistics.

3
00:00:09,107 --> 00:00:14,448
Skewness, kurtosis, outliers, and the mean, median and I Q R.

4
00:00:15,248 --> 00:00:17,667
The number hides the picture.

5
00:00:17,667 --> 00:00:22,589
Here are three distributions with the same kind of average.

6
00:00:22,589 --> 00:00:27,344
In the symmetric one the mean describes almost everybody.

7
00:00:27,344 --> 00:00:33,017
In the two-group one the mean lands in the gap and describes nobody.

8
00:00:33,017 --> 00:00:38,106
In the right-skewed one the mean sits above most of the bars.

9
00:00:38,106 --> 00:00:40,775
Same statistic, three realities.

10
00:00:40,775 --> 00:00:44,696
That is why we plot first and summarise second.

11
00:00:45,496 --> 00:00:49,468
Every distribution answers three questions.

12
00:00:49,468 --> 00:00:50,576
Where is it:

13
00:00:50,576 --> 00:00:54,732
the centre, measured by mean, median or mode.

14
00:00:54,732 --> 00:00:56,118
How wide is it:

15
00:00:56,118 --> 00:01:01,567
the spread, measured by range, standard deviation or I Q R.

16
00:01:01,567 --> 00:01:03,507
And what shape is it:

17
00:01:03,507 --> 00:01:06,832
skewness, kurtosis and stray points.

18
00:01:07,632 --> 00:01:11,327
Skewness tells you which way the tail points.

19
00:01:11,327 --> 00:01:18,963
Left-skewed data has a long tail on the left, like exam marks where most students score high.

20
00:01:18,963 --> 00:01:21,918
Symmetric data has both tails alike.

21
00:01:21,918 --> 00:01:29,472
Right-skewed data has a long tail on the right, like income, house prices and waiting times.

22
00:01:29,472 --> 00:01:32,592
The tail names the skew, not the hump.

23
00:01:33,392 --> 00:01:36,501
Skew pulls the mean but not the median.

24
00:01:36,501 --> 00:01:42,240
In right-skewed data, mode is less than median, which is less than mean.

25
00:01:42,240 --> 00:01:45,348
In left-skewed data the order reverses.

26
00:01:45,348 --> 00:01:50,928
This is why average income is higher than what a typical person earns:

27
00:01:50,928 --> 00:01:56,428
a few very large values sit in the tail and only the mean feels them.

28
00:01:56,428 --> 00:01:59,696
When the tail is long, report the median.

29
00:02:00,496 --> 00:02:04,826
Kurtosis measures how often extreme values happen.

30
00:02:04,826 --> 00:02:10,627
It is about the weight in the tails, not the sharpness of the peak.

31
00:02:10,627 --> 00:02:16,776
Excess kurtosis is kurtosis minus three, so a normal curve scores zero.

32
00:02:16,776 --> 00:02:21,625
Above zero means heavy tails and more outliers expected.

33
00:02:21,625 --> 00:02:24,136
Below zero means light tails.

34
00:02:24,936 --> 00:02:30,581
Platykurtic distributions have thin tails and extremes are genuinely rare.

35
00:02:30,581 --> 00:02:33,556
Mesokurtic is the bell curve benchmark.

36
00:02:33,556 --> 00:02:36,684
Leptokurtic distributions have fat tails:

37
00:02:36,684 --> 00:02:42,482
shocks and outliers are common, and standard deviation understates the risk.

38
00:02:42,482 --> 00:02:48,432
High kurtosis is a warning that your outlier check is about to find something.

39
00:02:49,232 --> 00:02:56,328
An outlier is a point far from the bulk of the data, and far needs a definition.

40
00:02:56,328 --> 00:03:01,118
The usual one is the one point five times I Q R fence.

41
00:03:01,118 --> 00:03:06,352
The I Q R is Q three minus Q one, the middle fifty percent.

42
00:03:06,352 --> 00:03:15,045
Points beyond Q one minus one point five I Q R, or Q three plus one point five I Q R, are flagged.

43
00:03:15,045 --> 00:03:21,343
Because the fence is built from quartiles, the outliers cannot move it.

44
00:03:21,343 --> 00:03:24,536
A flag is a question, not a verdict.

45
00:03:25,336 --> 00:03:28,842
There are three reasons a point sits far out.

46
00:03:28,842 --> 00:03:35,231
It may be a mistake, like an age of nine ninety nine; fix or remove it and say so.

47
00:03:35,231 --> 00:03:43,023
It may be rare but real, like a genuinely huge order; keep it, it is often the most interesting row.

48
00:03:43,023 --> 00:03:52,840
Or it may belong to the wrong population, like a wholesale buyer inside retail data; separate it and analyse the groups apart.

49
00:03:53,640 --> 00:03:58,980
Here is the toolkit sorted by what each statistic tells you.

50
00:03:58,980 --> 00:03:59,959
For centre:

51
00:03:59,959 --> 00:04:01,917
mean, median and mode.

52
00:04:01,917 --> 00:04:02,896
For spread:

53
00:04:02,896 --> 00:04:06,100
range, standard deviation and I Q R.

54
00:04:06,100 --> 00:04:06,990
For shape:

55
00:04:06,990 --> 00:04:08,949
skewness and kurtosis.

56
00:04:08,949 --> 00:04:12,865
The last column shows when each breaks down.

57
00:04:12,865 --> 00:04:20,163
The mean is dragged by one extreme value, while the median and I Q R rarely break.

58
00:04:20,163 --> 00:04:24,168
Always report a centre and a spread together.

59
00:04:24,968 --> 00:04:28,089
Seven monthly salaries, in thousands:

60
00:04:28,089 --> 00:04:30,535
twenty eight up to forty two.

61
00:04:30,535 --> 00:04:33,740
Now the manager joins at four hundred.

62
00:04:33,740 --> 00:04:41,838
The mean more than doubles and describes nobody in the room, while the median moves by one unit.

63
00:04:41,838 --> 00:04:46,898
A statistic that one row can move this far is not a summary.

64
00:04:46,898 --> 00:04:48,248
It is a hostage.

65
00:04:49,048 --> 00:04:50,896
So which do you report?

66
00:04:50,896 --> 00:04:57,887
If the histogram looks symmetric with no stray points, use mean and standard deviation.

67
00:04:57,887 --> 00:05:03,432
If the tail is long, use median and I Q R and say the data is skewed.

68
00:05:03,432 --> 00:05:10,504
If there are extreme points of unknown cause, report both and show how much they differ.

69
00:05:10,504 --> 00:05:14,200
And always put the picture next to the number.

70
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:17,561
Plot first, summarise second.

71
00:05:17,561 --> 00:05:21,093
Skewness says which side the tail is on.

72
00:05:21,093 --> 00:05:24,449
Kurtosis says how heavy the tails are.

73
00:05:24,449 --> 00:05:32,396
The one point five I Q R fence flags candidates; investigate them, do not sweep them away.

74
00:05:32,396 --> 00:05:40,344
Mean and standard deviation for tidy symmetric data, median and I Q R for everything else.

75
00:05:41,144 --> 00:05:44,517
Six mistakes cost marks and credibility.

76
00:05:44,517 --> 00:05:46,794
Deleting outliers silently.

77
00:05:46,794 --> 00:05:49,998
Reporting the mean of a skewed column.

78
00:05:49,998 --> 00:05:57,672
Using z greater than three on a small sample, where the ceiling is n minus one over root n.

79
00:05:57,672 --> 00:06:03,828
Treating a sentinel like zero, minus one or nine ninety nine as a number.

80
00:06:03,828 --> 00:06:07,623
Testing a hypothesis the same data suggested.

81
00:06:07,623 --> 00:06:12,008
And trusting one plot instead of at least two views.

82
00:06:12,808 --> 00:06:13,975
To take away:

83
00:06:13,975 --> 00:06:18,015
E D A is a habit through the whole lifecycle.

84
00:06:18,015 --> 00:06:23,131
Explore freely, confirm strictly, never on the same rows.

85
00:06:23,131 --> 00:06:28,966
A bigger sample shrinks noise; only a better sample removes bias.

86
00:06:28,966 --> 00:06:35,430
Summary statistics compress and compression loses structure, so plot it.

87
00:06:35,430 --> 00:06:37,943
Shape first, summary second.

88
00:06:37,943 --> 00:06:45,664
And median and I Q R survive contamination; mean, standard deviation and range do not.

89
00:06:45,664 --> 00:06:50,152
Next up is the lab on the Online Shoppers dataset.

